.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_FD 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_fd \- build a ccol_selectable from a raw file descriptor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro builds a ccol_selectable value, a tagged union whose type
here is ccol_selectable_fd, that wraps the file descriptor that you
give, with the I/O direction that you give.

  ccol_select_read; wake when the fd becomes readable (data available or
                     EOF / error condition)
  ccol_select_write; wake when the fd becomes writable (send buffer has room)

This macro expands to a compound literal, so it costs nothing at run
time.

ccol_select() never reads or writes the fd itself; it only reports
readiness (see the documentation of ccol_select()). When the read
direction wins, the caller then does its own read(2) or recv(2) on fd,
and when the write direction wins, its own write(2) or send(2). Either
call can find nothing, or get EWOULDBLOCK, when another reader or
writer wins the race first; POSIX select(2) itself has the same
time-of-check to time-of-use contract.

You can mix fd selectables and queue selectables in one ccol_select()
call. The library moves to a wait path built on epoll(7) (kqueue(2) on
FreeBSD) when any fd selectable is present, and uses the lighter
condition variable path when you give it queue selectables only.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
fd        An open file descriptor. It must be >= 0. A negative value
          makes a selectable that ccol_select() rejects with
          ccol_invalid_args.
dir       ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable value (not a pointer).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int pfd[2];
if (pipe(pfd) != 0) { perror("pipe"); exit(1); }  /* pfd[0] read, pfd[1] write */
int src = 42;
if (write(pfd[1], &src, sizeof(src)) != (ssize_t)sizeof(src)) {
    perror("write");
    exit(1);
}

/* Wake when the pipe read end has data OR q has a message: */
size_t idx;
ccol_retval_t r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_fd(pfd[0], ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_success && idx == 1) {
    /* the fd is ready; read it here */
    int val;
    if (read(pfd[0], &val, sizeof(val)) == (ssize_t)sizeof(val))
        printf("read %d\en", val);
}

/* Watch a socket for writability: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx, ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_write));
if (r == ccol_success && write(sock_fd, "hi", 2) < 0)
    perror("write");  /* EAGAIN: a different writer used the room */
.fi
